'''''Teucrium fruticans''''' ([[common name]] '''tree germander'''<ref name="GRIN">{{GRIN | access-date = 2008-06-05 }}</ref> or '''shrubby germander''')<ref name="NRCS">{{PLANTS
| taxon = Teucrium fruticans| symbol = TEFR3| access-date = 2008-06-05}}</ref> is a [[species]] of [[flowering plant]] in the mint [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Lamiaceae]], [[native plant|native]] to the western and central [[Mediterranean]]. Growing to {{convert|1|m|ft|0|abbr=on}} tall by {{convert|4|m|ft|0|abbr=on}} wide, it is a spreading [[evergreen]] [[shrub]] with arching velvety white shoots, glossy aromatic leaves and pale blue flowers in summer.

The Latin [[Binomial nomenclature|specific epithet]] ''fruticans'' means "shrubby" or "bushy".<ref name=RHSLG>{{cite book|last=Harrison|first=Lorraine|title=RHS Latin for gardeners|year=2012|publisher=Mitchell Beazley|location=United Kingdom|isbn=9781845337315|pages=224}}</ref>

The [[cultivar]] 'Azureum', with darker blue flowers, has gained the [[Royal Horticultural Society]]'s [[Award of Garden Merit]].<ref name = RHSPF>{{cite web|title=''Teucrium fruticans'' 'Azureum'|url=https://www.rhs.org.uk/Plants/74406/Teucrium-fruticans-Azureum/Details |publisher=Royal Horticultural Society|access-date=12 March 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.rhs.org.uk/plants/pdfs/agm-lists/agm-ornamentals.pdf | title = AGM Plants - Ornamental | date = July 2017 | page = 100 | publisher = Royal Horticultural Society | access-date = 1 December 2018}}</ref> It is hardy in milder areas, where temperatures do not fall below {{convert|-5|C|F|abbr=on}}. It prefers the shelter of a wall, in full sun with neutral or [[Soil pH|alkaline soil]].<ref name = RHSPF/>

